Here is a simple implementation of a singly linked list in TypeScript. This implementation includes methods for inserting nodes at the beginning and end of the list, as well as traversing the list from start to end.

```typescript
// LinkedList.ts
class Node<T> {
  /**
   * Node constructor
   */
  constructor(public value: T, public next?: Node<T>) {}
}

class LinkedList<T> {
  private head: Node<T> | null = null;
  private tail: Node<T> | null = null;

  /**
   * Insert a new node at the beginning of the list
   * @param value The value of the new node
   */
  public insertAtHead(value: T): void {
    const newNode = new Node(value);
    if (!this.head) {
      this.head = newNode;
      this.tail = newNode;
    } else {
      newNode.next = this.head;
      this.head = newNode;
    }
  }

  /**
   * Insert a new node at the end of the list
   * @param value The value of the new node
   */
  public insertAtTail(value: T): void {
    const newNode = new Node(value);
    if (!this.tail) {
      this.head = newNode;
      this.tail = newNode;
    } else {
      this.tail.next = newNode;
      this.tail = newNode;
    }
  }

  /**
   * Traverse the list from start to end and return a collection of values
   * @returns An array of node values
   */
  public traverse(): T[] {
    const values: T[] = [];
    let currentNode = this.head;
    while (currentNode) {
      values.push(currentNode.value);
      currentNode = currentNode.next;
    }
    return values;
  }
}

export default LinkedList;
```

Example usage:

```typescript
import LinkedList from './LinkedList';

const linkedList = new LinkedList<number>();

linkedList.insertAtHead(1);
linkedList.insertAtHead(2);
linkedList.insertAtTail(3);
linkedList.insertAtTail(4);

console.log(linkedList.traverse()); // [2, 1, 3, 4]
```

Architecture Choices:

*   The `Node` class is used to represent individual nodes in the linked list. Each node has a `value` property to store the node's value and a `next` property to point to the next node in the list.
*   The `LinkedList` class is the main class for the linked list implementation. It has methods for inserting nodes at the beginning and end of the list, as well as traversing the list from start to end.
*   The `insertAtHead` and `insertAtTail` methods create new nodes and update the `head` and `tail` properties of the list accordingly.
*   The `traverse` method uses a while loop to iterate through the list, pushing each node's value into an array.
*   The linked list implementation uses a singly linked list, where each node only points to the next node in the list. This design choice simplifies the implementation and reduces memory usage.

Type Safety:

*   The linked list implementation is type-safe, meaning that it checks the types of values inserted into the list at compile-time.
*   The `Node` class and `LinkedList` class are generic classes, which allows them to work with any type of data.

Memory Efficiency:

*   The linked list implementation uses a minimum amount of memory, as each node only points to the next node in the list.
*   The `head` and `tail` properties of the list are updated accordingly when nodes are inserted or removed, which helps maintain memory efficiency.
